---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
import { AFFILIATION, GITHUB_URL, LINKEDIN_URL, SITE_TITLE } from '../consts';
---

<Layout
	title={`About — ${SITE_TITLE}`}
	description="Who writes here, what he works on, and why the writing exists."
>
	<main>
		<header class="titleblock">
			<h1>{SITE_TITLE}</h1>
			<p class="affiliation">{AFFILIATION}</p>
		</header>

		<h2>What I do</h2>
		<p>
			I am an AI engineer at <a href="https://egeist.com.tr">Egeist Teknoloji</a> in İzmir.
			Enough of the job has turned out to be architecture rather than modelling that a fair
			amount of what I write here is about design.
		</p>
		<p>
			I have recently started graduate study, heading toward research. That means I now read
			papers with the intention of arguing with them rather than only applying them, and this
			site is where a good deal of that arguing ends up.
		</p>

		<h2>Why this site exists</h2>
		<p>
			I write to understand. Explaining a thing is the fastest way I know to find the places
			where I only thought I understood it — the step that seemed obvious until it had to be
			written down in order, the constant nobody bothers to justify. So the posts here tend
			to be written while the material is still difficult rather than after it stopped being.
		</p>
		<p>
			The consequence is that they are written for someone in my position rather than for
			someone who already knows. If a derivation is skipped, it is because I could genuinely
			do it in my head, not because it was tedious to type.
		</p>

		<h2>What I write about</h2>
		<p>
			Machine learning systems, mostly, and the theory underneath them. Occasionally
			something from the wider computer science that a problem dragged me into. Length
			follows the subject: some entries are a page of reading notes, some are a long
			derivation with the algebra left in.
		</p>

		<h2>Background</h2>
		<dl class="cv">
			<dt>2025 &ndash;</dt>
			<dd>
				<b>AI Engineer</b>, Egeist Teknoloji &mdash; İzmir. Building ORKIKS: AI core,
				LLM layer, and the backend architecture beneath both.
			</dd>

			<dt>2026 &ndash;</dt>
			<dd>
				<b>M.Sc. Computer Science</b>, Dokuz Eylül University. Thesis in progress:
				moving what is currently bought with runtime prompt text &mdash; persona,
				document faithfulness, tool use, flow discipline &mdash; into model weights,
				and measuring whether those capabilities survive together rather than one at
				a time. A direction rather than a result; it may still move.
			</dd>

			<dt>2021 &ndash; 2025</dt>
			<dd><b>B.Sc. Computer Science</b>, Dokuz Eylül University.</dd>
		</dl>

		<h2>Selected work</h2>
		<dl class="work">
			<dt><a href="https://hub.orkiks.com">ORKIKS</a></dt>
			<dd>
				Multi-agent AI platform, in production. Three specialized agents behind a LangGraph
				supervisor with intent routing; ReAct with per-SKU RAG over Postgres and pgvector;
				transactional-outbox persistence; a TypeScript storefront and BFF integrating
				Shopify, Trendyol and the WhatsApp Cloud API behind adapters.
			</dd>

			<dt><a href="https://github.com/Rfetha/Hukuk-SLM">Hukuk-SLM</a></dt>
			<dd>
				A Turkish legal small language model, in progress: a 9B Qwen-class model
				fine-tuned with QLoRA down to a 4-bit GGUF that fits 8&nbsp;GB of VRAM, evaluated
				on a
				four-axis suite — groundedness gating, abstention against hard-negative trap sets,
				and a dual LLM judge — over 21.5K grounded question–answer pairs synthesized from
				real statutes. The scope changed partway on a negative result worth reporting:
				supervised fine-tuning collapsed abstention from 0.74 to zero.
			</dd>

			<dt><a href="https://github.com/Rfetha/rfetha-skills">rfetha-skills</a></dt>
			<dd>
				A library of agent skills distilling software-design material — deep modules,
				domain-driven design, hexagonal architecture, the GoF and PoEAA patterns — into
				narrowly triggered, reusable form.
			</dd>
		</dl>
		<p class="more-work">
			The rest, including a PyTorch chess engine and a set of from-scratch computer-vision
			builds, is on <a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>.
		</p>

		<h2>Elsewhere</h2>
		<p>
			<a href={GITHUB_URL}>GitHub</a>{' · '}<a href={LINKEDIN_URL}>LinkedIn</a>
		</p>
	</main>
</Layout>

<style>
	.titleblock {
		text-align: center;
		margin-bottom: 1.7rem;
	}
	h1 {
		font-size: 1.55em;
		margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
	}
	.affiliation {
		font-style: italic;
		font-size: 0.82em;
		color: var(--muted);
		margin: 0;
	}
	dl {
		margin: 0;
	}
	.cv,
	.work {
		width: min(var(--fig), 100vw - 3rem);
		margin-left: 50%;
		transform: translateX(-50%);
	}
	.cv {
		display: grid;
		grid-template-columns: 7.5rem 1fr;
		gap: 0 1rem;
	}
	.cv dt {
		font-style: italic;
		font-size: 0.82em;
		color: var(--faint);
		font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
		padding-top: 0.32em;
	}
	.cv dd,
	.work dd {
		margin: 0 0 0.85rem;
		font-size: 0.92em;
		line-height: 1.55;
	}
	.cv dd b {
		font-weight: 400;
		font-family: var(--caps);
		letter-spacing: 0.045em;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	/* Why: repository names are identifiers. The small-caps face renders
	   `ORKIKS` full-height and `fin-cli` half-height off the same rule, which
	   both mismatches their real spelling and reads as inconsistent sizing. */
	.work dt {
		font-size: 0.95em;
		margin-top: 0.9rem;
		color: var(--ink);
	}
	.work dt a {
		color: var(--sig);
	}
	.more-work {
		font-size: 0.92em;
		color: var(--muted);
	}
	@media (max-width: 900px) {
		.cv,
		.work {
			width: 100%;
			margin-left: 0;
			transform: none;
		}
	}
	@media (max-width: 640px) {
		.cv {
			grid-template-columns: 1fr;
			gap: 0;
		}
		.cv dt {
			padding-top: 0.5rem;
		}
	}
</style>
